00:00:19,140 --> 00:00:22,100
Introducing the AI Agent Memory OS. EverOS is developed by the AI team at EverMind.

11
00:00:22,100 --> 00:00:24,490
An Open-Source Operating System for Agent Long-Term Memory

12
00:00:24,490 --> 00:00:28,250
The goal is to provide a memory infrastructure for self-evolving AI agents.

13
00:00:28,250 --> 00:00:31,210
So, for AI agents today, one of the biggest headaches they face...

14
00:00:31,210 --> 00:00:33,490
is its memory. For a quick chat, it's fine.

15
00:00:33,490 --> 00:00:35,530
But once you go beyond that context window,

16
00:00:35,530 --> 00:00:36,910
it has a memory like a goldfish.

17
00:00:36,910 --> 00:00:39,410
it forgets pretty much everything you've talked about before,

18
00:00:39,410 --> 00:00:41,920
So, with every new conversation, it has to start all over again.

19
00:00:41,920 --> 00:00:44,100
And that's exactly the layer EverOS aims to build.

20
00:00:44,100 --> 00:00:45,640
This allows the agent to remember,

21
00:00:45,640 --> 00:00:46,420
to build upon its knowledge,

22
00:00:46,420 --> 00:00:47,690
so it can reuse past experiences,

23
00:00:47,690 --> 00:00:49,630
Instead of having to start all over again from scratch every single time.

24
00:00:49,630 --> 00:00:51,900
And its core component is called EverCore.

25
00:00:51,900 --> 00:00:55,000
Its overall architecture is inspired by the human brain and how it processes information.

26
00:00:55,000 --> 00:00:56,080
It's split into four layers.

27
00:00:56,080 --> 00:00:58,380
the Infrastructure Layer, the Business Layer, and the Memory Layer.

28
00:00:58,380 --> 00:00:59,430
And the Agent Layer.

29
00:00:59,430 --> 00:01:00,570
So, to put it simply...

30
00:01:00,570 --> 00:01:01,970
So, after the agent finishes a task,

31
00:01:01,970 --> 00:01:04,000
EverOS will then conduct a review for it.

32
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:05,800
and consolidates that experience.

33
00:01:05,800 --> 00:01:07,380
so it's ready to go the next time.

34
00:01:07,380 --> 00:01:09,120
It’s not just about storing the data.

35
00:01:09,120 --> 00:01:10,800
but actually learning from it.

36
00:01:10,800 --> 00:01:13,860
And, EverOS is more than just a simple repository.

37
00:01:13,860 --> 00:01:16,480
Essentially, it's an umbrella project.

38
00:01:16,480 --> 00:01:19,320
The repository includes a set of architectural methods,

39
00:01:19,320 --> 00:01:20,940
a complete set of benchmarks,

40
00:01:20,940 --> 00:01:23,360
Plus, it provides over 25 use cases.

41
00:01:23,360 --> 00:01:25,400
So, I think the right way to actually get started with this is...

42
00:01:25,400 --> 00:01:28,100
is to pick a use case that interests you,

43
00:01:28,100 --> 00:01:29,900
For example, something like swarm intelligence agents,

44
00:01:29,900 --> 00:01:32,400
or maybe this Live2D character with memory.

45
00:01:32,400 --> 00:01:35,040
And then, you'd pick an architectural method.

46
00:01:35,040 --> 00:01:36,620
and then use benchmarks to compare them.

47
00:01:36,620 --> 00:01:38,140
Not only will you understand how to use EverOS,

48
00:01:38,140 --> 00:01:39,920
but also how EverOS is used in practice.

49
00:01:39,920 --> 00:01:44,220
And you can also learn from these real-world implementations how to actually apply agent memory.

50
00:01:44,220 --> 00:01:45,260
The use cases here...

51
00:01:45,260 --> 00:01:46,360
and a lot of them are really interesting.

52
00:01:46,360 --> 00:01:47,990
It's definitely worth checking out.

53
00:01:47,990 --> 00:01:49,810
And getting it up and running is also pretty straightforward.

54
00:01:49,810 --> 00:01:53,290


55
00:01:53,290 --> 00:01:55,490
Then, just install the dependencies, and you'll be up and running.

56
00:01:55,490 --> 00:01:56,970
Now, if you don't want to set it up yourself,

57
00:01:56,970 --> 00:01:59,270
They also offer EverOS Cloud, which you can use directly.

58
00:01:59,270 --> 00:02:01,229
So, if you don't want to go through all that trouble, this is the way to go.

59
